# Wealthfolio stack secrets (services VM) — single container
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   sops -e -i .sops.env
# (encryption needs only the public key in .sops.yaml, so no sudo and no age
# key are required to create this file — only editing one afterwards is.)
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/wealthfolio.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys —
# the stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# Reminder from CLAUDE.md: any value containing '$' must be single-quoted in
# the plaintext before encrypting. That is not theoretical here —
# WF_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH is MADE of '$'.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WF_SECRET_KEY — the master key. Derives BOTH the JWT signing key (session
# cookies) and the encryption key for /mnt/fast/wealthfolio/secrets.json,
# where the app stores market-data provider API keys.
#
# ⚠ ROTATING IT IS NOT A RE-LOGIN. Sessions dying is the trivial half; the
# real cost is that secrets.json becomes permanently undecryptable — every
# provider API key entered through the UI has to be re-entered. Portfolio
# data itself (wealthfolio.db) is NOT encrypted with it and survives.
#
# It must decode to exactly 32 bytes or the server refuses to boot
# ("WF_SECRET_KEY must be set and contain a 32-byte key") — which also means
# a deploy that races the decrypt (finding #11) crash-loops loudly instead
# of starting open or half-configured.
#
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32
# (base64 may contain '+' and '=': neither needs quoting; '$' cannot occur.)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WF_SECRET_KEY=changeme_openssl_rand_base64_32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WF_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH — the login password, as an Argon2id PHC string.
# This is the ONLY account on the instance (single-user by design); there is
# no username, no registration and no reset flow — recovery is editing this
# file, which is fine, because the hash is derived from the password and
# rotating it IS the password change. Rotate freely.
#
# 🚨 SINGLE-QUOTE IT IN THE PLAINTEXT — the vaultwarden ADMIN_TOKEN trap
# (finding #30), byte for byte: a PHC string is riddled with '$', compose
# interpolates env_file values, and an unquoted hash reaches the container
# as a DIFFERENT STRING. MEASURED at 3.7.0, the failure is LOUD: the hash
# is parsed at STARTUP and a string that is not a valid Argon2id PHC aborts
# the server — "Failed to parse WF_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH ... single-quote it
# or double every '$'" — so the trap is a crash-loop naming its own fix,
# not the quiet every-login-404s lockout of older auth.rs. The suite pins
# both this boot-fatal shape and the working round trip. Do not $$-escape
# instead; that advice is for inline `environment:` blocks.
#
# Generate (salt must be >= 8 chars; -e emits the PHC; printf, NOT echo,
# which can smuggle a trailing newline into the password):
#   printf '%s' 'your-password' | nix shell nixpkgs#libargon2 -c \
#     argon2 "$(openssl rand -hex 8)" -id -t 3 -m 16 -p 4 -e
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WF_AUTH_PASSWORD_HASH='changeme_argon2id_phc_keep_the_single_quotes'
